# DESCRIPTION

These are Plan 9's fcall(2) conversions, in Swift: a `Message` is a tag and an `Fcall`, one case a message type of 9p(5), with that type's fields.
`FcallType` names the type numbers; `QidType`, `OpenMode` and `DirMode` the bits of a qid's type, an open's mode and a stat's mode; `NineP` the protocol's constants: `noTag`, `noFid`, `maxWalk` (16), `ioHeader` (24, so a read or write carries <msize> − 24 bytes), `minMessage` and the version strings.

`encode` gives a message's bytes, its size first.
It returns nil for what can't be sent: a string or stat past 65535 bytes, a walk of more than 16 names or qids.

`decode` reads one message from exactly its bytes.
It refuses, with nil, bytes that are not exactly one message: a size that isn't theirs, a field that doesn't fit, a byte after the last field, a walk past 16, a type that doesn't exist (Terror, 106, among them), and a string that holds a NUL or isn't UTF-8.
On a stream, `size` reads the size the next message announces from its first four bytes, so a reader knows how many to wait for.

A `Dir` is a stat, its owners strings.
Rstat and Twstat carry a stat's bytes as `Dir.encode` gives them; a directory's Rread carries whole ones, back to back, which `decodeAll` reads.
`Dir.unchanged` is Twstat's template: every number all ones and every string empty, so a Twstat changes only the fields set in it.

Two messages, or two stats, are equal when their bytes are: a name is its bytes in 9P, so a name composed and the same decomposed are two names.

`Version.answer` is the server's half of Tversion: the dialect it speaks of the one asked, `9P2000.vx` (ours, when `extended`) or `9P2000` (for any other suffix, `9P2000.L` and `9P2000.u` among them) or `unknown`, and the smaller msize; nil when that msize is below `minMessage`.
A decoded message holds copies of its strings and data, not views of the buffer.
`dialect` names the dialect a version string asks for, or nil if it isn't 9P2000's.

